# TLS_MODE=dns-01 — ADVANCED, NOT plug-and-play out of the box. Read this
# whole comment before using it; README.md's TLS section has the same
# summary.
#
# This mode proves domain ownership via a DNS TXT record instead of an
# HTTP challenge, so — like custom-cert — it works even with ZERO inbound
# internet access. Unlike custom-cert, Caddy provisions and renews the
# certificate itself automatically; you never handle cert files by hand.
#
# The catch: this needs a Caddy build that includes a DNS provider module,
# and the stock `caddy:2` image this appliance ships does NOT include one —
# keeping this appliance's own "nothing to build" promise (see
# docker-compose.yml's own top comment) means we can't bundle every
# provider's module for everyone. To actually use this mode:
#
#   1. Get (or build with xcaddy) a Caddy image containing the module for
#      YOUR DNS provider — see https://caddyserver.com/docs/modules/ for
#      the growing list (Cloudflare, Route53, DigitalOcean, and dozens
#      more each have one).
#   2. In docker-compose.yml, point the caddy service's `image:` at that
#      image instead of the stock caddy:2.
#   3. Copy this file's content into a real file named Caddyfile.dns-01,
#      filling in your own provider's directive + credentials — the block
#      below is the Cloudflare shape, given here as one concrete, complete
#      example; swap `dns cloudflare ...` for your own provider's own
#      directive name and required arguments per its module's own docs.
#   4. Set TLS_MODE=dns-01 in .env.
{$DOMAIN} {
	tls {
		dns cloudflare {$CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN}
	}

	handle /internal/* {
		respond 404
	}

	handle /v1/* {
		reverse_proxy api:4000
	}

	handle {
		reverse_proxy dashboard:80
	}
}
